-- New-style daily words for February (days 32–59): a real devotion (paragraphs ending "Today: …"), a declaration and a prayer. Same KJV verse as before.
-- Only replaces days still in the old one-paragraph style, so anything rewritten in Admin → Devotions is kept.

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'John was an old man when he wrote this letter. He had walked with Jesus, seen Him die and seen Him risen. After a lifetime of following Him, this is what still amazed him: "Behold, what manner of love the Father hath bestowed upon us, that we should be called the sons of God."

"Behold" means stop and look. John is saying: do not rush past this.

God did not just forgive us and keep us at a distance, like a judge who lets a prisoner go. He brought us into His family. We are called His children, and in the next verse John adds: "now are we the sons of God." Not one day, but now.

Many people grew up without a father''s warmth. Some had fathers who were absent, harsh or hard to please. That can make it difficult to believe God really wants us as His children. But the Father has bestowed this love on us. It is His gift, freely given, not a reward we earn.

John also says the world does not recognise us, because it did not recognise Jesus. Your worth is not decided by how the world sees you. It is decided by whose child you are.

Today: whenever you pray today, begin with "Father". Say it slowly, and remember that He chose to call you His child.',
  declaration = 'Behold what love the Father has given me: I am called a child of God, and that is what I am.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You that You did not just forgive me, but brought me into Your family. Heal the places in me that find it hard to believe You want me. Let me live today as Your loved child. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy032' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'God spoke these words through Jeremiah to a people who had wandered far from Him and would soon go into exile. Jeremiah was known as the weeping prophet, and most of his messages were warnings. But here, God opens His heart: "I have loved thee with an everlasting love: therefore with lovingkindness have I drawn thee."

Everlasting love has no beginning that you caused and no end that you can cause. It did not start when you became good, and it will not stop when you fail.

And notice the word "drawn". God does not only wait for us to come back. He draws us, gently pulling us toward Himself, like someone drawing water from a deep well with a rope, or a parent drawing a crying child into a hug.

Later in the same chapter, God promises a new covenant: He will write His law on their hearts and remember their sin no more. His love was not only a feeling. It was a plan to bring them home.

Perhaps you feel you have wandered too far. God''s love is older than your wandering and stronger than your failures. He is drawing you even now. That pull you feel toward Him is His kindness.

Today: tell God honestly where you have drifted, and thank Him that His love has not let you go.',
  declaration = 'God has loved me with an everlasting love. With lovingkindness He is drawing me to Himself.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You that Your love for me is everlasting. Where I have drifted, draw me back with Your kindness. Help me rest in a love I did not start and cannot end. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy033' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In Romans 5, Paul explains that it is rare for someone to die even for a good person. Perhaps for a truly good person, someone might dare. But God showed His love in a completely different way: "while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us."

Not after we cleaned up. Not when we finally became worthy. While we were still sinners.

Human love often depends on what we see in the other person: their kindness, beauty, loyalty or usefulness. When they let us down, our love cools. But God''s love does not wait for us to deserve it. The cross is the proof.

"Commendeth" means God has shown it clearly, put it on display. When you doubt whether God loves you, do not look first at your circumstances, which can be confusing. Look at the cross, which is clear.

This also means God''s love is not fragile. If He loved you at your worst, He will not stop loving you now that you are His. Paul says in the next verses that if we were reconciled while we were enemies, how much more will we be saved now that we are His friends.

Today: when the thought comes that God could not love you because of something you did, answer it with this verse: "While I was still a sinner, Christ died for me."',
  declaration = 'God proved His love for me: while I was still a sinner, Christ died for me.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for loving me before I was worthy, while I was still a sinner. When I doubt Your love, turn my eyes to the cross. Let that love change how I see myself and how I love others. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy034' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Most of the book of Zephaniah is about judgment. But at the very end, the tone changes completely. God speaks to His people about a day of restoration, and this verse is one of the tenderest in the whole Bible.

"The LORD thy God in the midst of thee is mighty; he will save, he will rejoice over thee with joy; he will rest in his love, he will joy over thee with singing."

Picture it: the mighty God, the One who made the heavens, singing over you. Not shouting at you. Not sighing over you. Singing with joy.

"He will rest in his love" can also be read as "He will quiet you with His love", like a mother whose baby is crying. She does not scold the baby. She holds the child close and hums softly until the crying settles.

Many of us imagine God as always disappointed, waiting for us to do better. But this verse shows a God who takes delight in His people.

He is also "in the midst of thee". Not far away, but right in the middle of your situation, your home, your struggle.

Today: find a quiet moment, close your eyes and imagine God singing over you with joy. Let Him quiet your anxious heart with His love.',
  declaration = 'The Lord my God is with me and He is mighty. He saves me, rejoices over me, quiets me with His love and sings over me with joy.',
  prayer = 'Mighty God, thank You that You are in the middle of my life and that You delight in me. Quiet my restless heart with Your love. Help me believe that You sing over me with joy. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy035' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Just before these verses, Zion says, "The LORD hath forsaken me, and my Lord hath forgotten me." God''s people felt abandoned. And God answers with one of the strongest pictures of love we can imagine: a mother nursing her baby.

Can a nursing mother forget her child? Can she lose all compassion for the son she carried? It seems impossible. And yet, God says, even if that happened, "yet will I not forget thee."

Then He adds: "Behold, I have graven thee upon the palms of my hands." Not written in ink that fades, but engraved, cut into His hands. Some people write important phone numbers on their palm so they will not forget. God says your name is engraved on His.

When we read this as Christians, it is hard not to think of Jesus, whose hands were pierced for us, and who showed those scars to His disciples after He rose.

You may feel forgotten: overlooked at work, ignored by family, left behind while others move ahead. Even in church you may feel unseen. But you are not forgotten by God. You are always before Him.

Today: look at the palm of your hand and remember: "My name is engraved on God''s hands. He has not forgotten me."',
  declaration = 'God will never forget me. I am engraved on the palms of His hands.',
  prayer = 'Lord, when I feel forgotten, remind me that You have engraved my name on Your hands. Thank You for Jesus, whose hands were pierced for me. Comfort me with Your faithful love today. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy036' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Romans 8 begins with "no condemnation" and ends with "no separation". In between, Paul talks honestly about suffering, groaning and weakness. He does not pretend that life with God is easy.

Then he finishes with these words: "For I am persuaded, that neither death, nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor powers, nor things present, nor things to come, Nor height, nor depth, nor any other creature, shall be able to separate us from the love of God, which is in Christ Jesus our Lord."

Look at how complete the list is. Death cannot separate us. Neither can life, with all its pressures. Spiritual powers cannot. Things happening now cannot. Things that will happen in the future cannot. And just in case anything was missed, Paul adds: "nor any other creature." Nothing in all creation.

Paul was "persuaded". This was not a wish. It was a settled conviction, formed through shipwrecks, prisons, beatings and rejection. He had been through almost everything on this list, and God''s love held.

What are you afraid could separate you from God? A past sin? A present struggle? A future you cannot control? None of these can break His hold.

Today: write down the thing you fear most. Then write underneath: "Even this cannot separate me from the love of God in Christ Jesus."',
  declaration = 'Nothing can separate me from the love of God in Christ Jesus: not death, not life, not anything now or to come.',
  prayer = 'Father, I am persuaded that nothing can separate me from Your love. When fear comes, remind me of this truth. Hold me fast in Christ, today and always. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy037' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Paul wrote Ephesians from prison, and in chapter 3 he kneels and prays for the believers. He asks that they may be rooted and grounded in love, and then that they may comprehend "the breadth, and length, and depth, and height" and "know the love of Christ, which passeth knowledge."

It is a strange prayer: to know a love that is beyond knowledge. Paul is saying we can know it truly, but never completely. There will always be more.

Think of standing on the beach at Lagos and looking at the ocean. You can see it, touch it, swim in it. You truly know the ocean. But you could never measure all of it from where you stand. God''s love is like that.

Its breadth is wide enough to reach every person and every nation. Its length stretches from eternity past to eternity future. Its height lifts us into God''s presence. Its depth reaches into the lowest places of shame, pain and failure.

Notice also that Paul prays we would know it "with all saints". We grasp God''s love best together, as we hear each other''s stories and see His love at work in different lives.

Today: ask someone, a friend, a family member or someone at church, to tell you about a time they experienced God''s love. Listen, and let it enlarge your view.',
  declaration = 'I am rooted and grounded in love. I am growing to know the love of Christ, which is wider, longer, higher and deeper than I can measure.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, root me and ground me in Your love. Open my eyes to see how wide, long, high and deep it is. Fill me with all the fullness of God, and let me grasp Your love together with Your people. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy038' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'John was called "the disciple whom Jesus loved". In his old age he wrote this letter, and love is everywhere in it. Here he says: "Beloved, let us love one another: for love is of God; and every one that loveth is born of God, and knoweth God."

Notice where love comes from. It is "of God". It begins with Him. A few verses later John says, "God is love", and "We love him, because he first loved us."

This changes how we think about loving people. We often treat love as something we must produce by our own effort, like squeezing water out of a dry sponge. And with difficult people, we soon run dry.

But real love flows from God through us. Our job is to stay connected to the source. The more we receive His love, the more we have to give.

John also says love is evidence. People who love show that they are born of God and know Him. Not perfect love, but real love that grows.

He calls his readers "Beloved" before he tells them to love. That is the order. First you are loved. Then you love.

Today: think of one person who is hard to love. Before you see them, pray: "Lord, You love me. Let Your love flow through me to them." Then show them one act of kindness.',
  declaration = 'Love is of God. I am loved by Him, so I can love others with the love He gives me.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for loving me first. Fill me with Your love today, especially for the people I find hard to love. Let my love for others show that I am Your child and that I know You. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy039' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Jesus spoke these words on the night before He died. He had just washed His disciples'' feet and shared the Passover meal with them. He knew the cross was hours away. And He gave them this command: "love one another, as I have loved you."

Then He explained what that love looks like: "Greater love hath no man than this, that a man lay down his life for his friends."

Within a day, He would do exactly that.

The standard is not "love one another as much as you feel like it", or "love those who love you back". It is "as I have loved you": serving, forgiving, patient, sacrificial love.

Most of us will never be asked to die for someone. But we are asked to lay down our lives in smaller ways every day. A mother lays down her sleep for a sick child. A husband lays down his pride to apologise first. A friend lays down a free evening to sit with someone who is grieving. A worker lays down the chance to cheat because it would hurt others.

Jesus also called His disciples friends, not servants. Our love for each other is meant to reflect the friendship He offers us.

Today: look for one way to lay down something for someone else today: your time, your comfort, your right to be right. Do it quietly, for Jesus.',
  declaration = 'I love others as Jesus has loved me. I lay down my life in daily acts of love for His friends.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, thank You for laying down Your life for me. Teach me to love others the way You have loved me. Show me where I can lay down my comfort, time or pride for someone today. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy040' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = '1 Corinthians 13 is often read at weddings, and it is beautiful there. But Paul first wrote it to a church full of problems: divisions, pride about spiritual gifts, people suing each other and selfish behaviour at the Lord''s Supper. He put this chapter right in the middle of his teaching about spiritual gifts, as if to say: without love, all your gifts are just noise.

Then he describes love, not as a feeling, but by what it does and does not do. "Charity suffereth long, and is kind; charity envieth not; charity vaunteth not itself, is not puffed up, Doth not behave itself unseemly, seeketh not her own, is not easily provoked, thinketh no evil."

Love is patient with slow and difficult people. Love is kind, actively doing good. Love does not envy when others are blessed. Love does not boast or show off. Love is not rude. Love is not selfish. Love does not get angry easily. Love does not keep a record of wrongs.

Try reading the list with your own name in place of "charity". It quickly shows where we need grace.

Now read it with "Jesus" in place of "charity". It fits perfectly. That is the love He has for you, and the love He wants to grow in you.

Today: choose one line from this list to practise today, in one specific relationship.',
  declaration = 'God''s love in me is patient and kind. It does not envy, boast or seek its own, and it keeps no record of wrongs.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I see how far my love falls short of this. Thank You that Your love for me is exactly like this. Grow this patient, kind, humble love in me, starting with the people I live and work with. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy041' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Paul ends his description of love with four strong words: love "Beareth all things, believeth all things, hopeth all things, endureth all things."

This is love that holds on.

"Beareth all things" can also mean love covers or protects. It does not expose people''s weaknesses to embarrass them. It shields.

"Believeth all things" does not mean love is foolish or naive. It means love chooses to believe the best about people, giving them the benefit of the doubt instead of assuming the worst.

"Hopeth all things" means love does not give up on people. When someone keeps failing, love still hopes God can change them. Many parents pray for wayward children for years because love keeps hoping.

"Endureth all things" means love keeps going through hardship. It stays when it would be easier to leave.

This is not a call to stay in danger or to excuse abuse. Love also tells the truth and seeks what is truly good for the other person. But in ordinary relationships, with all their friction and disappointment, love keeps holding on.

This is how God loves us. He covers our sin, believes in what He is making us, hopes for us and endures with us.

Today: think of someone you are close to giving up on. Ask God for fresh hope for them, and pray for them by name.',
  declaration = 'God''s love in me bears all things, believes all things, hopes all things and endures all things.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for never giving up on me. Give me Your enduring love for the people in my life, especially those I am tempted to give up on. Help me cover, believe, hope and endure, with wisdom and truth. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy042' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Romans 12 is full of practical instructions about how believers should live together. In this verse Paul uses a word for family love: the natural warm affection between parents and children, brothers and sisters. "Be kindly affectioned one to another with brotherly love."

Church is not meant to be like a bus station, where strangers sit next to each other for a while and then go their separate ways. It is meant to be a family.

Then Paul adds a challenge: "in honour preferring one another." Another way to say it is: outdo one another in showing honour. Compete, not to be honoured, but to honour others.

In many cultures, including ours, people care deeply about honour and respect: titles, seating positions, who is greeted first. Paul turns this upside down. Instead of fighting for honour, Christians are to give it away.

This looks like celebrating someone else''s promotion without jealousy. Giving the best seat to someone else. Speaking well of people when they are not in the room. Thanking the cleaners and the ushers, not only the pastor. Noticing the person who is always overlooked.

Jesus did exactly this. He washed His disciples'' feet.

Today: find one person to honour today: a word of thanks, public praise, a small gift or simply treating them with special respect.',
  declaration = 'I love my brothers and sisters in Christ with warm family affection, and I outdo others in showing honour.',
  prayer = 'Lord, give me a family heart toward Your people. Free me from competing for honour. Help me notice and honour others, especially those who are often overlooked, just as Jesus did. In His name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy043' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Proverbs has a lot to say about friends, both good and bad. This verse describes the best kind: "A friend loveth at all times, and a brother is born for adversity."

"At all times" includes the good times and the hard times. Some friends are only around when things are going well: when you have money, success or something they need. When trouble comes, they disappear.

A true friend stays. A true brother or sister seems to be made for the hard days. They show up at the hospital. They help when your business fails. They do not leave when you are grieving or when your reputation is damaged.

Jonathan was that kind of friend to David. When King Saul turned against David, Jonathan risked his own position and his relationship with his father to protect him.

This verse invites two questions. First, do you have friends like this? If you do, thank God for them. Second, are you a friend like this? Are you only around for the good times, or do you show up in adversity?

Jesus is the perfect friend who loves at all times. He did not leave us in our worst moment. He came closer.

Today: think of a friend who is going through a hard time. Reach out today: a call, a visit, a message or a practical gift. Let them know they are not alone.',
  declaration = 'I am a friend who loves at all times. In adversity I show up for the people God has given me.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for the friends who have stood with me in hard times. Make me that kind of friend to others. Show me who needs me today, and give me courage to show up. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy044' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Today many people celebrate love. Some feel happy and loved. Others feel lonely, forgotten or reminded of what they have lost.

This verse speaks to both. "A man that hath friends must shew himself friendly: and there is a friend that sticketh closer than a brother."

The first part is practical. Friendship takes effort. If we want friends, we need to be friendly: to reach out, to greet, to invite, to listen, to remember birthdays, to check in. Many lonely people are waiting for others to come to them. Sometimes the first step is ours.

The second part is a beautiful promise. There is a friend who sticks closer than a brother. Family relationships are meant to be the strongest, but sometimes even family lets us down. This friend never does.

Christians have long seen Jesus in this verse. He is the friend of sinners. He called His disciples friends. He never leaves, never forsakes and loves at all times.

So whether today feels full of love or empty, you are not without a friend. Jesus sticks closer than a brother, and His love does not depend on romance, relationship status or anyone else''s attention.

Today: thank Jesus for being your faithful friend. Then show yourself friendly to someone who might be feeling lonely today.',
  declaration = 'Jesus is the friend who sticks closer than a brother. I am never without His love, and I will show myself friendly to others.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, thank You for being my faithful friend. Whatever I feel today, I am loved by You. Help me show friendship to someone who feels alone, and let Your love reach them through me. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy045' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'The writer of Ecclesiastes looked at a man who worked endlessly, with no family and no friend, always chasing more money but never satisfied. He asked, "For whom do I labour?" Then he wrote: "Two are better than one; because they have a good reward for their labour. For if they fall, the one will lift up his fellow: but woe to him that is alone when he falleth; for he hath not another to help him up."

God did not design us to do life alone. Two are better than one in work, in travel, in warmth and in protection. And then he adds: "a threefold cord is not quickly broken."

Think of someone walking a dark road at night and falling into a gutter. If they are alone, they might lie there for a long time. If someone is with them, help is immediate.

Many people today are surrounded by others but still alone. We have hundreds of contacts but nobody we would call at 2am. We go to church but leave without anyone really knowing us.

Spiritual life also needs companions: someone to pray with, someone who will be honest with you, someone who will notice when you start to drift.

Christians often see the third strand of the cord as God Himself, binding two people together in Him.

Today: name one person you can walk closely with in faith. Reach out and suggest praying together, reading a plan together or simply checking in weekly.',
  declaration = 'Two are better than one. God gives me companions to lift me up, and I will lift others up when they fall.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You that I was not made to walk alone. Give me faithful companions on this journey, and make me a faithful companion to others. Be the third strand that holds us together. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy046' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Naomi had lost everything: her husband and both her sons, in a foreign land. She decided to go home to Bethlehem and told her two daughters-in-law to go back to their own families. Orpah kissed her and left. But Ruth clung to her and said: "whither thou goest, I will go; and where thou lodgest, I will lodge: thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God."

Ruth was a Moabite, a foreigner, a young widow with no reason to follow a bitter old woman to a land where she might be rejected. But she chose loyalty and love. And she chose Naomi''s God.

That decision changed history. In Bethlehem, Ruth met Boaz, married him and became the great-grandmother of King David. Through that line came Jesus.

Ruth''s love was costly. It was not based on what she would gain, but on commitment. The Hebrew idea behind her kind of loyalty is often called hesed: faithful, covenant love.

Some relationships need this kind of commitment: a marriage going through a dry season, an elderly parent who needs care, a friend who has become difficult. Ruth''s story reminds us that faithfulness in hidden places can be part of something much bigger than we can see.

Today: think of someone God is calling you to be faithful to, even when it is costly. Ask Him for Ruth''s kind of love.',
  declaration = 'I choose faithful love and loyalty. God sees my hidden faithfulness and weaves it into His bigger story.',
  prayer = 'Lord, give me Ruth''s kind of love: loyal, humble and faithful even when it costs me. Help me stay committed to the people You have placed in my life, and trust You with the bigger story. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy047' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 68 celebrates God as the One who rides through the heavens in power. Yet in the middle of these images of might, we find a tender description of who He cares for: "A father of the fatherless, and a judge of the widows, is God in his holy habitation. God setteth the solitary in families."

The mighty God pays attention to the lonely.

"The solitary" means the one who is alone: the person who moved to a new city for work, the widow whose children live far away, the young person who feels like nobody understands them, the single adult who goes home to an empty room every night.

God''s answer to loneliness is family. Sometimes that means a husband, wife or children. But very often it means the family of God: a church, a small group, a home fellowship where people know your name and notice when you are missing.

Jesus said that whoever does the will of His Father is His brother, sister and mother. In Christ, nobody is meant to be an orphan.

If you feel alone, God sees you and wants to place you in a family. If you are not alone, God may want to use you to welcome someone who is.

Today: if you are lonely, ask God to lead you to His people, and take one step toward them. If you are not, look around your church or street for someone who is alone, and invite them in.',
  declaration = 'God sets the lonely in families. In Christ I belong, and I will make room for others to belong too.',
  prayer = 'Father of the fatherless, thank You that You see the lonely. Where I feel alone, place me among Your people. Where I am surrounded by love, open my eyes and my home to someone who is solitary. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy048' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 133 is one of the songs of ascents, sung by pilgrims as they travelled up to Jerusalem for the great feasts. People came from many tribes and towns, and as they walked together, they sang: "Behold, how good and how pleasant it is for brethren to dwell together in unity!"

Then the psalm gives two pictures. Unity is like the precious oil poured on Aaron''s head when he was anointed high priest, running down his beard onto his robes. And it is like the dew of Mount Hermon falling on the hills of Zion, bringing life to dry ground.

Unity is anointing. It flows from the head down. Unity is refreshing. It brings life where things were dry.

The psalm ends: "for there the LORD commanded the blessing." God places His blessing where His people live in unity.

Unity does not mean everyone is the same, or that we never disagree. Twelve tribes walked to Jerusalem, each different. Unity means we choose to love, honour and work together because we belong to the same Lord.

Division is easy. Gossip, pride, offence and competition break churches, families and friendships. Unity takes humility.

Today: is there a relationship in your family, church or workplace where unity has broken down? Pray for it, and take one humble step toward peace.',
  declaration = 'I will walk in unity with God''s people. Where there is unity, God commands the blessing.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I want to be a peacemaker, not a divider. Heal the broken unity in my family, my church and my relationships. Give me humility to take the first step toward peace. Command Your blessing where we walk together. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy049' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In Ephesians 4, Paul describes the old way of living that believers have left behind and the new way they are called into. Just before this verse, he lists things to put away: bitterness, wrath, anger, clamour, evil speaking and malice. Then he says: "be ye kind one to another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another, even as God for Christ''s sake hath forgiven you."

Kindness is practical goodness: helping, encouraging, giving. Tenderheartedness is the inner softness that feels for others instead of becoming hard and cold. Forgiveness is releasing people from the debt they owe us for what they did.

And the reason is clear: "even as God for Christ''s sake hath forgiven you." We forgive because we have been forgiven. Not because the other person deserves it, but because we did not deserve it either.

Jesus told a story about a servant who was forgiven a huge debt by his master, then went out and seized a fellow servant who owed him a small amount. The master was angry. When we refuse to forgive, we forget how much we ourselves have been forgiven.

Forgiveness does not mean pretending nothing happened, or always trusting someone again immediately. It means choosing not to hold the debt against them, and leaving justice to God.

Today: think of one person you are holding something against. Pray: "Lord, You forgave me. I choose to release them." Repeat it as often as you need to.',
  declaration = 'I am kind, tenderhearted and forgiving, because God for Christ''s sake has forgiven me.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for forgiving me so much. Soften my heart where it has become hard. Help me release the people who have hurt me, and fill my words and actions with kindness. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy050' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In Micah 6, the people ask a big question: how can we please God? Should we bring burnt offerings, thousands of rams, rivers of oil, even our firstborn child? They thought pleasing God was about bigger and bigger religious offerings.

God''s answer is simple and searching: "He hath shewed thee, O man, what is good; and what doth the LORD require of thee, but to do justly, and to love mercy, and to walk humbly with thy God?"

Three things. Do justly: be fair and honest in how you treat people, in business, at work, in your family, in the community. Pay workers fairly. Do not cheat with scales or prices. Defend the weak.

Love mercy: not just show mercy occasionally, but love it. Enjoy being kind. Delight in giving people second chances.

Walk humbly with your God: stay close to Him, depend on Him, and do not think more of yourself than you should.

God is not impressed by big religious displays if our daily lives are unjust and proud. A loud prayer meeting does not cancel a dishonest business deal.

The good news is that this kind of life is not reserved for pastors or prophets. Anyone can live it: the trader, the student, the civil servant, the farmer, the mother at home.

Today: ask yourself, "Where can I act more justly, show more mercy and walk more humbly today?" Choose one concrete step.',
  declaration = 'I do justly, I love mercy and I walk humbly with my God.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I do not want an empty religion. Help me act justly in my work and dealings, love mercy in my relationships and walk humbly with You every day. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy051' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Proverbs 15 begins with this simple but powerful observation: "A soft answer turneth away wrath: but grievous words stir up anger."

Most of us know this is true. A harsh reply is like pouring petrol on a fire. A gentle reply is like pouring water.

But it is hard to practise. When someone shouts at us, insults us or accuses us unfairly, everything in us wants to answer back, and louder. In a family argument, in a traffic jam, in a WhatsApp group, the temptation is to win.

A soft answer is not a weak answer. It takes more strength to stay calm than to explode. It does not mean we never speak the truth or never disagree. It means we choose our tone and words carefully, so that we can be heard.

Gideon is a good example. After a great victory, the men of Ephraim were angry that he had not called them sooner. Instead of arguing, he humbly praised what they had done. The Bible says their anger toward him subsided.

Jesus was the perfect example. When He was insulted, He did not insult in return. He trusted His Father who judges righteously.

Today: when someone speaks harshly to you, pause before you answer. Take a breath, pray a quick prayer, and choose a gentle reply.',
  declaration = 'My words are gentle and wise. A soft answer from me turns away wrath.',
  prayer = 'Lord, guard my mouth today. When I am provoked, help me pause and answer gently. Use my words to calm conflicts, not to stir them up. Make me more like Jesus in how I speak. In His name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy052' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In Colossians 3, Paul describes believers putting on new clothes, like someone changing out of dirty work clothes into clean ones. He lists compassion, kindness, humility, meekness and patience. Then he says: "Forbearing one another, and forgiving one another, if any man have a quarrel against any: even as Christ forgave you, so also do ye. And above all these things put on charity, which is the bond of perfectness."

"Forbearing" means putting up with one another. In every family, church and workplace, people annoy each other. We have different habits, opinions and personalities. Forbearing means we do not make every small irritation into a war.

Forgiving goes further. It deals with real wrongs: when someone has a genuine complaint against another. And again the standard is Christ: forgive as He forgave you.

Then Paul says: above all these, put on love. Love is like the belt that holds all the other clothes together. Without love, compassion becomes pity, humility becomes weakness and patience becomes cold endurance. Love binds everything into a beautiful whole.

Every morning we choose what clothes to wear. Every day we also choose what attitudes to put on.

Today: as you get dressed this morning, pray: "Lord, help me put on patience, forgiveness and, above all, love." Then wear them into your home and work.',
  declaration = 'I put on patience and forgiveness, and above all I put on love, which binds everything together.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, You forgave me freely. Help me bear with others'' weaknesses and forgive their wrongs as You forgave mine. Clothe me with Your love today, and let it hold everything else together. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy053' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 103 is David talking to his own soul: "Bless the LORD, O my soul, and forget not all his benefits." He lists them: forgiveness, healing, redemption, love, mercy, renewal. Then he gives two pictures of God''s mercy.

First, height: "as the heaven is high above the earth, so great is his mercy toward them that fear him." Look up at the sky on a clear night. You cannot measure how high it goes. God''s mercy is like that.

Second, distance: "As far as the east is from the west, so far hath he removed our transgressions from us." If you travel north long enough, you eventually start going south. But you can travel east forever and never start going west. The two never meet.

That is how far God has moved our sins from us. He does not keep them in a drawer to bring out later. He does not hang them on the wall to remind us.

Many believers still carry sins God has already forgiven. They keep confessing the same old sin, feeling the same old shame. But if you have confessed it and turned to Him, it has been removed.

The enemy will try to bring it back. Your memory might replay it. But God''s word stands: as far as the east is from the west.

Today: if an old forgiven sin keeps haunting you, say aloud: "That sin has been removed as far as the east is from the west." Then thank God for His mercy.',
  declaration = 'God''s mercy toward me is higher than the heavens. He has removed my sins as far as the east is from the west.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for mercy greater than I can measure. Thank You that my confessed sins are gone, removed as far as the east is from the west. Help me stop carrying what You have already taken away. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy054' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Peter wrote this letter to believers facing suffering and pressure. He told them the end of all things was near, so they should be clear-minded and prayerful. And then: "above all things have fervent charity among yourselves: for charity shall cover the multitude of sins."

"Fervent" means stretched out, intense, eager. Not a lukewarm, polite love, but a love that strains toward others.

Then comes the reason: love covers a multitude of sins. Peter is echoing Proverbs, which says hatred stirs up strife, but love covers all sins.

This does not mean love hides evil or protects abusers. Serious wrongs still need to be faced honestly and justly. But in everyday life, people constantly offend each other: forgetting things, speaking carelessly, being moody, making mistakes. Love does not record every offence. It does not broadcast other people''s faults to everyone. It covers, the way a mother puts a cloth over a sleeping child.

Imagine a church where every small offence was announced, debated and remembered forever. It would quickly fall apart. Love keeps communities together by letting many small things go.

God does this for us. He covered our sins through Jesus. Now He asks us to cover others with love.

Today: if you hear about someone''s mistake, choose not to spread it. If someone offends you in a small way, choose to cover it with love and let it go.',
  declaration = 'Above all, I love fervently. My love covers a multitude of sins and keeps peace among God''s people.',
  prayer = 'Lord, fill me with fervent love for Your people. Help me cover small offences instead of broadcasting them. Make me a person who protects, restores and builds up, just as You have done for me. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy055' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In Galatians 6, Paul talks about believers who are caught in sin, and others who are carrying heavy loads. He says: "Bear ye one another''s burdens, and so fulfil the law of Christ."

A burden is a weight too heavy for one person to carry alone. It might be grief, sickness, debt, a difficult child, a failing business, depression or a long struggle with temptation.

Bearing someone''s burden means coming alongside and taking some of the weight. Sometimes that is practical: food, money, transport, childcare, fixing something. Sometimes it is emotional: listening without judging, sitting with them in silence, crying with them. Sometimes it is spiritual: praying with them, standing with them in faith.

Paul calls this "the law of Christ". Jesus said His new commandment was to love one another as He loved us. And He carried our heaviest burden, our sin, on the cross.

A few verses later Paul says that each person shall bear his own load. These two ideas fit together. We each take responsibility for our own lives, but when the weight becomes crushing, we help each other.

Think of people carrying a heavy table up the stairs. One person alone would strain and drop it. Four people carry it easily.

Today: ask God to show you one person carrying a heavy burden. Offer one practical way to help carry it this week.',
  declaration = 'I help bear the burdens of others, and so I fulfil the law of Christ.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, You carried my heaviest burden on the cross. Open my eyes to people around me who are struggling under heavy loads. Give me compassion and practical ways to help carry their burdens. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy056' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Paul wrote 1 Thessalonians to a young church facing persecution and grief. Some of their loved ones had died, and they were worried. Paul reminded them of the hope of Christ''s return. Then he said: "Wherefore comfort yourselves together, and edify one another, even as also ye do."

"Comfort" means to come alongside and encourage. "Edify" means to build up, like building a house brick by brick.

Notice that Paul says, "even as also ye do." They were already doing it. He was not scolding them, but encouraging them to keep going. Even encouragement needs encouragement.

Words have power. A careless word can discourage someone for years. A kind, honest, faith-filled word can lift someone for a long time. Many people can still remember something encouraging a teacher or pastor said to them decades ago.

Building up is not the same as flattering. Flattery says nice things that are not true, to get something. Encouragement says true things in a way that strengthens someone toward God.

In a world full of criticism, complaints and bad news, a believer who consistently encourages others is a gift. Your church, family and workplace need builders, not demolishers.

Today: send three short messages of encouragement to three different people. Tell them something specific you appreciate about them, or a verse you are praying for them.',
  declaration = 'I comfort and build up the people around me. My words strengthen others toward God.',
  prayer = 'Lord, make me an encourager. Let my words build others up, not tear them down. Show me who needs encouragement today, and give me the right words to strengthen them in You. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy057' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'On the night before His death, Jesus washed His disciples'' feet, including the feet of Judas, who would betray Him. After Judas left, Jesus said: "A new commandment I give unto you, That ye love one another; as I have loved you, that ye also love one another. By this shall all men know that ye are my disciples, if ye have love one to another."

Why was it new? The command to love your neighbour was already in the law of Moses. What was new was the standard: "as I have loved you." Humble, serving, forgiving, sacrificial love, the kind that washes feet and goes to the cross.

And this love is the mark of a disciple. Not big buildings, impressive programmes or powerful preaching, good as they may be. People will know we belong to Jesus by how we love each other.

In the early church, outsiders were amazed at how Christians cared for each other, and for others too: sharing food, caring for the sick, welcoming people from every background.

Sadly, the world also notices when Christians fight, gossip and divide. Our love, or lack of it, speaks loudly about Jesus.

Today: ask God, "How can I love my brothers and sisters in Christ the way Jesus loves me?" Do one humble act of service for another believer today.',
  declaration = 'I love others as Jesus loved me. By my love, people will know I am His disciple.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, You washed feet and laid down Your life. Teach me to love like You. Let my love for Your people be so real that others see You in it. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy058' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In Romans 13, Paul lists some of the commandments: do not commit adultery, do not kill, do not steal, do not bear false witness, do not covet. Then he sums them all up in one sentence: "Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself." And he concludes: "Love worketh no ill to his neighbour: therefore love is the fulfilling of the law."

If you truly love your neighbour, you will not steal from them, lie about them, take their spouse or plot against them. Love keeps the commandments from the inside.

Many people think of God''s commands as a list of restrictions. But Paul shows that they are a description of love. Each command protects something precious: life, marriage, property, truth.

Love is not only about avoiding harm. It is about actively seeking good. But this verse starts with something basic: love works no ill. It does not harm.

That challenges us. Do our words harm others? Do our business practices harm customers? Do our habits harm our families? Does our silence harm people who need us to speak up?

As February comes to an end, after a month of reflecting on love, remember that love is not just a feeling. It is a way of living that seeks the good of others and refuses to harm them.

Today: think about one area where your actions might be harming someone, even unintentionally. Ask God to help you change it.',
  declaration = 'Love fulfils God''s law in me. My love does no harm to my neighbour, but seeks their good.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for showing me what love looks like this month. Search my heart and show me where I may be harming others. Help me love my neighbour as myself, and fulfil Your law through Your love in me. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy059' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;
